  <prompt>
    Define the versioned operation contract that is the spine of R4 — the single schema both the client
    (ProseMirror step→operation interceptor) and the server (Patch Engine) implement IDENTICALLY (FR-11).
    The op types are a CLOSED set: `insertText`, `deleteRange`, `replaceRange`, `setMark`, `clearMark`,
    `splitParagraph`, `mergeParagraph`, `insertParagraph`, `deleteParagraph`, `setBlockAttr`. Every op is
    anchored `{paraId, runIndex, offset|range}` and the envelope carries a schema **version** field. Borrow
    Slate's discriminated-union op shape (`{type, path/anchor, offset, properties}`) — see
    `notes/bridge-prior-art.md` finding #2 — as the SHAPE, not the identity model (our durable key is
    `w14:paraId`, which Slate lacks).

    Author it in two places that mirror each other exactly:
    - SERVER: `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Compose/Operations/ComposeOperation.cs` (+ related op-type
      records and a schema-version constant). Pure C# records; no AI internals, no Graph types.
    - CLIENT: `src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/types/compose-operations.ts`, EXTENDING the
      existing `src/types/compose-contracts.ts` (do not fork or duplicate the contracts module).

    The schema is the single source both ends VALIDATE against; a serialized op MUST round-trip
    client→server→client without loss. Do NOT bake in AI-internal types (ADR-013) or Graph types (ADR-007).
    This task does NOT implement capture or patch — it defines and validates the CONTRACT only.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Contract architect fluent in both .NET 8 records (discriminated-union-style with a `type` discriminator +
    `JsonPolymorphic`/`JsonDerivedType` or an explicit tag) and TypeScript discriminated unions. Understands
    the R4 anchor model `(paraId, runIndex, run-local-offset)` (D2) and that this schema is the one artifact
    both ends must agree on byte-for-byte in serialization.
  </role>

  <goal>
    A versioned, closed-set operation contract implemented identically on client and server: server records
    under `Services/Compose/Operations/` and a client discriminated union under
    `src/types/compose-operations.ts` (extending `compose-contracts.ts`), covering all ten op types each
    anchored `{paraId, runIndex, offset|range}` with a schema-version field on the envelope. A serialized
    operation log round-trips client→server→client with no loss; both ends validate against the same schema.
    No AI-internal or `Microsoft.Graph` types appear in the server contract (Tier-1 NetArchTest stays green).
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      D1 (design §0) locks step-level operational deltas as the R4 delta model; FR-11 (spec) names the closed
      op-type set and the `{paraId, runIndex, offset|range}` anchor + a version field. This is the spine both
      the client interceptor (task 020) and the server Patch Engine (task 030) compile against; the offset→run
      addressing table (task 011) resolves the fine anchor at patch time. Prior art: `notes/bridge-prior-art.md`
      finding #2 (Slate's small closed discriminated-union op set — the cleanest public schema to mirror; borrow
      the SHAPE, not Slate's session-only `path` identity — our durable key is `w14:paraId`). The existing
      client contracts live in `src/types/compose-contracts.ts` — EXTEND it, do not duplicate. The old
      text-/paragraph-coarse contracts (`DocxAnnotation.TargetText`, `ComposeEditedParagraph {paraId,text}`)
      are retired; this schema replaces both (spec §New Components).
    </background>
