    <patterns>
      <pattern name="Slate closed discriminated-union op set" location="projects/spaarkeai-compose-r4/notes/bridge-prior-art.md finding #2">
        `{type, path, offset, properties}` — mirror the closed-set discriminator shape; substitute `w14:paraId` for Slate's session-only `path` as the durable key.
      </pattern>
      <pattern name="paraId+runIndex+run-local-offset anchor" location="projects/spaarkeai-compose-r4/notes/bridge-prior-art.md finding #4/#6">
        A bare (paraId, char-offset) drifts under a same-paragraph earlier edit; the durable anchor is (paraId, runIndex, run-local-offset), run boundaries re-derived on apply. Encode offsets as run-local, never absolute.
      </pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Context + rigor declaration">Output the FULL rigor-level declaration (🔒 RIGOR LEVEL: FULL). Load bridge-prior-art findings #2/#4/#6, design §0 D1/D2, spec §FR-11, and the existing `compose-contracts.ts`. State the Placement Justification (contract stays in `Services/Compose/`; cites `.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md`).</step>
    <step order="1" name="Design the closed op-type set">Enumerate the ten op types + the `{paraId, runIndex, offset|range}` anchor + the schema-version field. Confirm each op can be expressed by the anchor alone; if any cannot, STOP and escalate before finalizing (see escalation).</step>
    <step order="2" name="Author the server contract">Write `Services/Compose/Operations/ComposeOperation.cs` (+ related op-type records + a schema-version constant) as pure C# records with a `type` discriminator. No AI-internal, no Graph types.</step>
    <step order="3" name="Author the client contract">Write `src/types/compose-operations.ts` as a discriminated union mirroring the server shape; extend `compose-contracts.ts` (import/augment, no duplication).</step>
    <step order="4" name="Round-trip validation">Add round-trip validation: a server unit test (`tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests/Services/Compose/`) and a client Vitest asserting a serialized op log round-trips client→server→client without loss. Confirm both ends validate against the same schema.</step>
    <step order="5" name="Guards + build">Build the BFF and the shared lib; run the Tier-1 NetArchTest facade check (no AI internals in `Services/Compose/`). Report BFF publish size absolute + delta vs ~49.63 MB (zero new runtime package expected).</step>
    <step order="6" name="Close out">Update `projects/spaarkeai-compose-r4/tasks/TASK-INDEX.md`: set task 003 status to ✅. Document any op-type shape decision or deviation in `projects/spaarkeai-compose-r4/notes/`.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>An op type in the closed set CANNOT be expressed by the `{paraId, runIndex, offset|range}` anchor alone (e.g., a structural op that needs a cross-paragraph reference the anchor can't carry) — STOP and surface it before finalizing the schema; a mis-shaped op propagates drift into every downstream task.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>Two coarse contracts overlap conceptually: `DocxAnnotation` (text-anchored, retired) and `ComposeEditedParagraph {paraId,text}` (paragraph-coarse, retired). Verified in notes/as-built-inventory.md.</existing>
    <extension>Cannot extend either — both are text-/paragraph-coarse; the op schema is the new paraId+offset spine that supersedes both. The client side DOES extend the existing `compose-contracts.ts` module (no fork).</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Without a single shared op contract, client and server cannot agree on ID-anchored edits; the two-path drift that caused Bug A returns and structural edits remain impossible.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

